00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.690
community to solve next
generation data processing

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.100
problems.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.400
First, let's create
a foundation.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.930
Let's frame the discussion
of next generation data

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.470
processing.

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.350
So I work in Seattle,
and Frances and I

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:13.640
were given a template.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.400
And the slides may
look somewhat familiar

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.480
at this point in the day.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.450
And I had one thought when I
saw this slide-- I don't have

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.650
enough vitamin D, and
I am definitely working

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.800
in the wrong Google office.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.150
I absolutely want to work there.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.730
It's gorgeous.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.659
But if I were you, what
I would be wondering

00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:32.700
is-- and we're talking
about next generation data

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.700
processing.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.410
What things specifically,
what challenges

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:39.720
do we need to solve for in
order to end up in a space

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.420
where we can say we are in next
generation data processing?

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.740
We think there are
three specific things.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.510
Now, infrastructure
can sometimes be hard.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.520
Now, creating unified, batch,
and streaming data processing

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.830
pipelines can be challenging.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.740
Now, the portability
of those data pipelines

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.770
can be a real pain.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.720
First, management.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.245
Now, the emergence of open
source data processing

00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:10.990
tools that allow you to
process large amounts of data

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.770
in parallel-- the Apache Hadoop
and Apache Spark ecosystems

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:20.060
are a great example-- they're
both a blessing and a curse.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.230
They allow you to set
up an environment where

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.860
you can decide on a tool, you
can read the documentation,

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.560
you can get your
infrastructure, you can then

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.190
set that infrastructure up,
you can set up your tools.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.230
But then you have
to tune the tools.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.000
You often have to
find experts who

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.130
can help you adjust all of
the knobs on those tools.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.460
You have to productionalize
your environment.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.740
Even though the tools
exist, and they're awesome--

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.115
they're very
powerful and they're

00:03:46.115 --> 00:03:48.160
great to use-- they
can be very difficult.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.640
And by the end of the
day, you are probably

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.090
now a team of people
supporting both infrastructure

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.510
and software when your end
goal at the end of the day

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.700
was to simply process a lot of
data that's important to you.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.630
But next, this should no longer
be the case in the future.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.500
We think it should be
very similar to using

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.230
a tool in the toolbox.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.170
You should be able
to open the toolbox,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.240
pick out the tool you need,
not have to assemble the tool

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.659
and forge the iron
and tune everything.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:18.200
You should know how to use the
tool intuitively, or even just

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.140
watch a simple YouTube
video to get it,

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:24.050
and then carry away
and solve your problem.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.550
FRANCES PERRY: Now,
the second thing

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.924
that we think is
really important

00:04:26.924 --> 00:04:28.360
for the future of
data processing

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.830
is the right set of
abstractions, how you represent

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.250
your data processing pipeline.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.710
As data processing needs have
become more sophisticated,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.780
users often find that they have
to write a traditional batch

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.240
system for correct
results and also

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.170
use a different streaming system
to get more real time updates.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.210
Now, this bifurcation
means you have

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.990
to write twice as much code.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:53.650
You have to maintain twice
as much infrastructure

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.280
and somehow reconcile
these two different results

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.750
into one conceptual output.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.520
Now, we think if we can find the
right way to represent our data

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.640
processing pipelines, we can
handle both batch and streaming

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.890
use cases in the
same unified system.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.490
And the final
thing that we think

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:14.150
is going to change the future of
data processing is portability.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.000
Many current systems
tightly couple

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.100
the code for expressing your
logic with the implementation

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.910
of the service itself.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.540
So that means if you want
to move your application

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.950
from Hadoop to Spark or
from on-premise to cloud,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.940
you have to rewrite
large portions

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.860
of that logic in a
different framework,

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.720
and then you have to dive in
and find what the right tuning

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.020
knobs are in this new scenario.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.050
So we can break that cycle.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:42.440
Again, if we find that right
abstraction, that right model,

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.530
you'll be able to write your
application logic once and run

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:46.460
it multiple places.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.160
JAMES MALONE: So ultimately,
you might be asking, great.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.490
So you're identified three
things-- management, model,

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:55.910
and portability.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:56.910
But why do these matter?

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.190
Why are these the things
that you have picked?

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.520
Why are these the problems
that we need to solve for,

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.790
and what are the impacts on
me today and in the future?

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.687
I'm going to use a metaphor.

00:06:07.687 --> 00:06:08.770
Say you have a rental car.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.603
And many of you may
have gotten a rental car

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:11.730
when you traveled here.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.950
You trust that the rental car
agency has managed the car.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.620
You don't have to pop the
hood and check the oil

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:21.320
level every time you
get a new rental car.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.822
You also are familiar with
the controls of a car,

00:06:23.822 --> 00:06:26.280
so you don't need to learn a
new set of controls every time

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.621
you get a rental car.

00:06:27.621 --> 00:06:29.620
And while you're traveling
in the United States,

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.280
most signs are
relatively the same,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.390
and you trust that
the car will drive

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.684
on different types of roads.

00:06:34.684 --> 00:06:35.850
So you have the portability.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.220
You can drive on the freeway.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.020
You can drive on country roads.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:41.270
You can drive on city streets.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.100
There's no problems there.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.410
Ultimately, why
it matters to you

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:48.100
is when you are wrangling
with the infrastructure

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.580
and the tooling to solve
complicated data problems,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.036
you are focusing on the
infrastructure and the tooling,

00:06:55.036 --> 00:06:56.660
and you're not focusing
on what matters

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:59.100
to you the most-- your data.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.640
These are not new challenges.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.140
And in fact, a lot
of the challenges

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.610
have existed for
quite some time.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.370
They're really hard
problems to solve, however.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:08.980
So let's start with
a little history.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.690
Because MapReduce is
a solution to some

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.690
of these problems
which has influenced

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.599
the present and
potentially the future.

00:07:15.599 --> 00:07:16.640
FRANCES PERRY: All right.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.570
So back in the early days,
Google, like many other folks,

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:22.000
needed to process a lot of data.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.740
However, conventional
tools and methods

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.360
couldn't keep up with
the growth of this data,

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.410
so engineers ended up
spending their time

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.500
writing custom computations
to handle each particular use

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.480
case.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.410
Now, if there's one
thing engineers hate,

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.130
it's wasting time,
particularly on something

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:39.730
that can be automated.

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.620
So the engineers started looking
for a generalized solution

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:45.020
for specifying logic
that runs in parallel

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.890
across many machines.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.510
At the same time, they wanted to
shield the author of that logic

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.190
from dealing with
low-level details,

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.620
like moving data from
one machine to another

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.690
and handling fault tolerance.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.940
So Jeff and Sanjay settled
on a simple reusable pattern.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.800
You take your data set, and
you partition it into chunks.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.490
Then you take each
chunk and hand it

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.620
out to a different
mapping machine.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.510
A mapping machine
takes its chunk

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:12.670
and runs a piece of
user-provided logic

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.560
over that chunk to
process each element.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.370
Now, each of those chunks
is processed independently

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.150
on a different machine, so
this makes it extremely easy

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.010
to parallelize.

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.280
Once the mapping
machines are done,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.560
they redistribute their
data across multiple

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.170
reducing machines based
on a key of the data.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:33.230
And then finally, each reducing
machine-- again, independently

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:37.520
from other reducing machines--
runs another bit of user logic

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.750
to create these final outputs.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:43.100
Together, this is your
output collection.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.470
Now, the beautiful
thing about this pattern

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.640
is it's extremely powerful.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.900
You can take pretty much
any big data processing

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:53.039
algorithm that you want, and
you can run it in MapReduce.

00:08:53.039 --> 00:08:54.580
You might have to
take your algorithm

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:57.030
and completely convolute
it beyond any recognition,

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.780
but you can shove it
into that framework

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.450
if you try hard enough.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:05.060
So in 2004, Google published
a paper on MapReduce.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.350
And at this point, things
diverge a little bit.

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:10.970
Inside Google, we
kept innovating.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.250
But we were doing this in
a relatively homogeneous

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:16.160
environment, so we
had only a few file

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.870
formats we were dealing with.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:19.440
We had a limited
number of languages

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.220
that we needed to
support, and we had

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.550
extremely standardized tooling.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.200
So this meant that we got
to spend our time focusing

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.105
on really refining
our core methodology

00:09:28.105 --> 00:09:30.470
and generating new
ideas on how we would

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:33.140
want to process this data.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:35.920
So we continued to publish
papers on these new ideas.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:39.250
And from the external community,
folks saw a lot of PDFs

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.557
popping up-- a lot of
good things to read,

00:09:41.557 --> 00:09:43.390
but nothing concrete
that you could actually

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:46.030
get your hands on and play with.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:49.450
Now, at the same time,
externally in the open source

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:52.330
community, we had a different
implementation of MapReduce

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.525
inside Hadoop.

00:09:53.525 --> 00:09:57.800
And an entire data processing
ecosystem developed here.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.450
Some of this continued to be
published by the Google papers,

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.300
but there was also a
lot of new innovation

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:06.490
that happened here on its own
in this very diverse system.

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:09.190
So today, Google Cloud
Platform contains

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.980
two systems, two products
that come from this MapReduce

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.070
lineage.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:15.580
The first one is
Google Cloud data flow.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:17.930
And this is a
fully-managed cloud service

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:19.780
that evolved directly
from MapReduce

00:10:19.780 --> 00:10:21.940
and includes some of these
later ideas from Google,

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.110
like FlumeJava's clean
abstractions and MillWheel's

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:28.660
focus on streaming and
real-time execution.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:31.050
Now, for those users who
have a significant investment

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.990
in open source
technologies already,

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:34.780
we have Google Cloud
Dataproc, which

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.200
is a fully-managed
service for executing

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.800
Hadoop in Spark programs.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:39.300
James.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:42.730
JAMES MALONE: Thank you.

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:45.021
So let's take a look at the
first product in the Google

00:10:45.021 --> 00:10:47.870
Cloud Platform portfolio
that's based directly-- quite

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:51.990
literally-- on MapReduce--
Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:56.500
So as Frances mentioned,
the Apache Hadoop ecosystem

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.760
evolved directly as
an implementation

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.580
of the MapReduce white paper.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:05.410
And arguably, it's had just--
I apologize-- unarguably,

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:09.580
it has had an enormous impact
on the current state and future

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:10.850
of data processing.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:13.630
It allows people to solve
really hard data problems that

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.210
weren't possible before.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:16.240
It has the models.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.690
It has tools.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:19.270
It has the community.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:23.160
It's really made a change
in how people process data.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.400
But a lot of these tools
also come with their own set

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.190
of challenges.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:29.750
And this is true
for Apache Spark.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.650
It's true for Apache Hadoop.

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:33.600
In general, a lot
of open source tools

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.890
share these three
common problems.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.910
First, installation--
they can be a real pain

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.770
to install and configure.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:43.540
Second, they can be
really tricky to run

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.690
at a cost-effective scale.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:49.080
Third, typically, you
have to know quite a bit

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.860
about all of these
different components

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:52.860
to use them effectively.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.960
Well, Google has been running
MapReduce and other data

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:58.260
processing technologies
at scale for a long time.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:02.010
So why not leverage all of
this existing investment

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:05.490
infrastructure and knowledge
to create a product?

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.290
Well, we did, and
Cloud Dataproc is

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:09.690
the answer to that challenge.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:13.270
It brings the familiar
Spark and Hadoop ecosystem

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:14.910
to the Google Cloud platform.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.894
This is great for
moving existing code

00:12:16.894 --> 00:12:19.310
that you've already developed
in on-premise or other cloud

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:21.272
environments or a
brand new development.

00:12:21.272 --> 00:12:23.730
And if you're familiar with
the Spark and Hadoop ecosystem,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.750
there's a wealth
of different tools

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:28.950
that fit into that ecosystem.

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:32.720
Cloud Dataproc ultimately helps
solve the thorniest management

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:35.860
headaches that usually follow
the Apache Spark and Hadoop

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:37.070
ecosystem.

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:40.890
It really focuses on
speed, simplicity, and cost

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:42.480
effectiveness.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.790
Cloud Dataproc is designed
as an ephemeral solution.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.010
And ultimately, what
we mean by that is we

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.140
want you to have clusters
when you need them,

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:52.090
when you actually
have work to do,

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:53.930
and we want you to be
able to turn them off

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:55.240
when you don't need them.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:58.800
We don't want you to have to
carry excess idle capacity when

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:00.580
it makes no sense to you.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.360
So we like to think
of Cloud Dataproc

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:04.100
as combining the
power of Google,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:08.230
both in software and hardware,
with the power of open source.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.790
So ultimately, what
is Cloud Dataproc?

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:16.100
It's best to think about
Cloud Dataproc as two

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:20.296
top-level concepts--
clusters and jobs.

00:13:20.296 --> 00:13:22.145
Cloud Dataproc
clusters ultimately

00:13:22.145 --> 00:13:25.780
are a layer cake of
three big components.

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:28.120
The first-- Cloud Dataproc
is built on the power

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:29.550
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.210
This means the virtual machines
that you use for your cluster,

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:34.370
our Google Compute
Engine machines,

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.270
you can connect to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:39.870
So we use the already-existing
tools available to us.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:42.590
We then have an agent that
runs on top of this cluster.

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:45.580
It's really the glue between
the underlying Google Cloud

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:49.570
Services and the open source
tools that live on a cluster.

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:51.300
The agent is doing
all of the things,

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.625
like tuning the 500 to 600
different knobs that exist

00:13:54.625 --> 00:13:56.710
in Spark and Hadoop alone.

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:59.360
And finally, we have
the open source tools.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:00.110
This is Spark.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:00.800
This is Hadoop.

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:01.425
This is Hive.

00:14:01.425 --> 00:14:04.460
This is Pig.

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:07.360
The second top-level concept
in Cloud Dataproc are jobs.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:09.040
And we provide a
set of tools which

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.660
abstract the pain of trying to
manage workflows away from you

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.370
to create an
abstraction layer where

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.190
you can run some of
the most popular job

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:19.600
types on your cluster without
having to open terminal windows

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.660
or know how Spark
Submit works, which

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:25.320
means you can submit Spark,
PySpark, Spark SQL, Pig, Hive,

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:28.420
Java MapReduce, all
without actually having

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:30.210
to ever log into a cluster.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.850
You can simply create
a cluster in one click

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.790
and run your job in
your second click.

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:37.464
That's it.

00:14:37.464 --> 00:14:39.790
Cloud Dataproc is
also not isolated.

00:14:39.790 --> 00:14:41.080
It doesn't stand alone.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.850
And this is a very
important point,

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:45.950
because it means that you
can read and write data

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:47.810
to many different places.

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.630
You can read and write data
from other GCP products.

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.820
You can read and write
data to Dataproc jobs,

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:54.950
both on the same
cluster, or since it's

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:57.950
an ephemeral solution and you
can create clusters quickly,

00:14:57.950 --> 00:14:59.420
any other cluster.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:01.400
You could also feed data
to other applications

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:02.025
on the cluster.

00:15:02.025 --> 00:15:04.770
So we give you the ability
to install other open source

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:07.200
components-- for example,
Apache Zeppelin on the cluster--

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.870
so your spark can be talking
to Zeppelin all on one cluster

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.350
easily.

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:14.300
I want to go to the
point about Dataproc

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:17.270
talking to other Google
Cloud Platform products,

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.795
because this is a
very important point.

00:15:19.795 --> 00:15:23.130
It allows you to use the
familiar Spark and Hadoop

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:26.600
ecosystem that you may already
have knowledge, existing code,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.150
documentation,
tooling, and use it

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:32.130
with the other powerful tools
in the Google Cloud Platform

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:33.940
portfolio.

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:36.730
For example, you could
have a PySpark job

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:39.170
which reads data from
Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:42.570
processes the data with Spark
using PySpark and potentially

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.650
all of your favorite
Python libraries,

00:15:44.650 --> 00:15:47.706
and then output the resultant
data set to BigQuery.

00:15:47.706 --> 00:15:49.080
Google Cloud
Dataproc is designed

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.000
to bring the
experience and power

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.790
of this very familiar
ecosystem to the integrated and

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:55.460
comprehensive
processing platform

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:58.740
provided by Google
Cloud Platform.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.870
I want to back up some
of the things I'm saying

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:04.619
and not just say that Google
Cloud Dataproc is fast, easy,

00:16:04.619 --> 00:16:05.410
and cost effective.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:06.868
I want to give some
specific points

00:16:06.868 --> 00:16:09.300
to actually elaborate
on why that is the case.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.540
So let's start with speed.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:13.200
On average, Cloud
Dataproc clusters

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:16.680
start in about 90 seconds
for your average cluster.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:18.440
They also stop in
about the same time.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:19.650
That's really important.

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.816
Because if you have an
ephemeral cluster that you're

00:16:21.816 --> 00:16:23.610
creating precisely
when you need it,

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:25.840
it means you spend less time
waiting for your cluster

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:27.030
to become available.

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:30.550
You also aren't paying for
your cluster to start and stop.

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.820
Second, clusters can
be resized at any time.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:36.320
So if you have a series of big
jobs, you can add more nodes.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:38.111
When you're done, you
can remove the nodes.

00:16:38.111 --> 00:16:40.320
And Cloud Dataproc will
handle all of the brokerage

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.290
in trying to commission
and decommission nodes

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.780
without causing
your jobs to fail.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:46.580
We also don't
bloat the clusters.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:49.920
So we include vanilla versions
of Spark, Hadoop, Hive,

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:52.000
Pig, and a few other
components, but we're not

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.620
putting on software extras
that we think you might not

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:57.880
need which are going to slow the
cluster down or just complicate

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.770
your use of the cluster.

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:04.180
Ease of use-- so as I mentioned
when I was talking about jobs,

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:07.180
we provide a job wrapper
which allows you to send jobs

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:09.150
to the cluster, monitor jobs.

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.740
You can stream the
output of drivers

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:14.295
without ever opening
an SSH window.

00:17:14.295 --> 00:17:15.420
You can do it in a browser.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:16.630
You can do it with Python.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:19.800
You can do it with any
number of libraries.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.530
You can do through a REST API.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:24.589
That also means we have
several surface areas

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:26.750
that you can use to interact
with Cloud Dataproc,

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:30.670
including our Google Developers
console, the Google Cloud

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:33.450
SDK, REST APIs, and
at the end of the day,

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:35.180
since this is built
on Compute Engine

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.320
and it is vanilla Spark and
Hadoop, if you want to go SSH

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:39.880
into clusters, go wild.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.300
You totally can.

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:45.150
And finally, optimized for
cost-- I have never really met

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:48.750
a Spark or Hadoop job which runs
specifically to one hour or two

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:49.930
hours or three hours.

00:17:49.930 --> 00:17:53.490
Typically, it's a fraction
or a subset of hours.

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:55.850
We bill Cloud
Dataproc by the minute

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:57.380
with a 10-minute minimum.

00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:00.240
This means that if your
job runs for 28 minutes,

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.780
you pay for 28
minutes, not one hour.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.580
And when you're creating large
clusters with potentially tens

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:09.320
or hundreds of nodes, this
can be absolutely substantial.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:10.910
We also priced
Cloud Dataproc based

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:13.680
on the size of your cluster
and the number of virtual CPUs.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:17.080
So it's easy to understand, and
doing the cost calculation is

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:18.640
fairly straightforward.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.950
And finally, you can
use preemptables and now

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:24.400
custom machine types as
part of our GA of last month

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.400
that allow you to
very carefully craft

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.900
the size of your cluster based
on the needs of your data

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:30.410
processing pipeline.

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:32.580
That means that you can
articulate your cluster

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:37.500
specifically so you aren't over
or under-consuming resources.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.256
So going back to
the three things

00:18:39.256 --> 00:18:40.630
that Frances and
I are discussing

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:42.750
today that are really
important problems that

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.690
need to be solved for
next generation data

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:47.030
processing-- Cloud Dataproc
addresses one of them

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:48.350
head on-- management.

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.930
We have really tried to
solve the thorny management

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:53.850
problems that exist with
some of the open source tools

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:55.910
so you don't have
to worry about them.

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:58.660
But there are other services
in the Google Cloud platform

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:00.545
portfolio that go a
little bit further,

00:19:00.545 --> 00:19:04.012
and Cloud Dataflow
is one of them.

00:19:04.012 --> 00:19:05.220
FRANCES PERRY: Thanks, James.

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:08.830
So let's dive in to more
details on Cloud Dataflow.

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:10.935
Now, this is the path
that came out of MapReduce

00:19:10.935 --> 00:19:13.490
and is based on a bunch
of continued innovation

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:15.060
internally at Google.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:17.270
Now it's available to
you as a cloud product,

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:20.020
and there are two key
advantages to Cloud Dataflow.

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.460
The first benefit is
the programming model.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:24.040
This is the way
that we encourage

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:26.710
you to write dataflow programs.

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:29.340
This is the way that you
represent your business logic,

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.840
and you focus on that--
your application logic,

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:36.100
not how you cram that logic into
some underlying infrastructure.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:38.470
Instead, you're
building a pipeline.

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:42.800
Your pipeline contains a mix of
custom element-wise processing,

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:46.030
aggregations, and
library functions.

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.530
Dataflow pipelines can process
both bounded, fixed size data

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:52.340
sets, like we're used to in
traditional batch systems,

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.880
but it can also handle
infinite streams of data.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.020
And when you're writing
that application logic that

00:19:58.020 --> 00:19:59.750
becomes your Dataflow
pipeline, you

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:02.260
don't have to wonder about
what type of collection

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:03.150
you're running over.

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:04.880
Your logic will
apply equally well

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.930
to both bounded and
unbounded data sets.

00:20:07.930 --> 00:20:10.240
This means that you have
a lot of flexibility

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:12.680
in how you write
your logic, and it

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.160
can be reusable in many
different scenarios.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:19.800
Now, to truly unify batch and
streaming into a single system,

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:22.410
we need to separate the
notion of event time

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:24.470
from processing time.

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:27.950
Event time is a time an
event actually happened.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:31.820
So let's say we have a
mobile gaming application.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.400
Users are on their cellphones
all over the world,

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:35.620
frantically doing something.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:37.862
They're popping
balloons, crushing candy,

00:20:37.862 --> 00:20:39.070
whatever it is they're doing.

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:40.450
It's happening everywhere.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:43.660
And we want to process those
points of their scoring

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:46.100
and figure out
information about that.

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:50.170
So the time the user crushed the
candy-- that's the event time.

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:52.100
The processing time
is the time when

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:55.920
that score is available in
our system for processing.

00:20:55.920 --> 00:20:57.800
Ideally, those would
be the same thing,

00:20:57.800 --> 00:20:59.660
but distributed systems
never cooperate.

00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:03.560
So there's actually
usually quite a difference.

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.030
So here, we've got a
sample data set of points

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:09.470
that a specific team
scored in our game.

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:12.170
So on the x-axis, you
can see the event time.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:14.540
Again, this is the time
the point was scored.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:17.360
And on the y-axis, we
have processing time.

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:21.520
So if distributed systems
weren't actually a problem,

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.940
everything would appear
along this dashed line.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.880
Something would happen,
and immediately, you

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.600
would be able to process
it in our system.

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:30.290
Sometimes, things aren't so bad.

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:33.820
So this event here happened
right before 12:07.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:36.810
And just after 12:07, it's
available in our system

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:37.790
for processing.

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:41.280
So maybe there was just
a small network delay.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:42.650
Other times, it's worse, though.

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:45.400
So this event over here
is seven minutes delayed

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:48.610
from when it happened
before we can process it.

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:51.970
Perhaps this user was playing
our game in an elevator

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:55.160
or in a subway, somewhere
where they had a temporary lack

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:57.290
of network connectivity.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:00.190
We had to wait for this user
to reestablish a connection

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:03.210
before that event was able
to be ingested to our system

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:04.860
and processed.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:07.550
The margins of this
graph aren't nearly wide

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:10.860
enough to contain what would
happen if our game supported

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:13.150
an offline mode.

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.830
Now we could have users
playing our mobile game

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:18.750
on a transatlantic flight
while they're in airplane mode.

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:22.730
It might be hours until
users land, reconnect,

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:26.510
and those events are
available for processing.

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:28.900
So as you can start
to see, these types

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:31.200
of infinite
out-of-order data sets

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.780
are really, really
tricky to reason about--

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:37.120
unless you know the
right questions to ask.

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:39.210
The dataflow model
itself revolves

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:41.310
around four key questions.

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:44.900
The first one is what
results are calculated.

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:47.420
What is that algorithm
that you're actually doing?

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:51.380
Are you computing sums, joins,
histograms, machine learning

00:22:51.380 --> 00:22:53.800
models?

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:56.650
Where in event time
are results calculated?

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:59.770
So how does the time when
each event actually occurs

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:01.910
affect your overall results?

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:04.740
Are we aggregating
events over all time,

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.000
for fixed windows
of time, or based

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:10.150
on bursts of user activity?

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:14.140
When in processing time
are results materialized?

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:16.580
So that's asking, does the
time each element arrives

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:19.120
in the system and actually
get available for processing,

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:21.120
does that affect results?

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:23.040
How do we know when
to emit results?

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.920
What do we do when
data comes in late

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:27.430
and we've already
emitted the result?

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:31.470
And finally, the fourth question
is how do refinements relate.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:34.457
So if we start emitting multiple
results because new values are

00:23:34.457 --> 00:23:36.040
coming in and we're
changing our mind,

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:38.170
what do we do with each
of those new copies?

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:40.820
How do they interact?

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:43.800
So let's take a look at how we
use these questions to build

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.700
a simple pipeline.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:47.790
So here's a snippet
from our pipeline.

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:50.660
We are processing those scoring
results from our mobile gaming

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:51.980
application.

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:53.870
In yellow, you see
the computation

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:57.210
that we're performing, the
first question, the what.

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:59.640
Here we're taking
team score pairs

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:01.830
of these scores that
happened, and we're

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.010
going to sum them per team.

00:24:03.010 --> 00:24:06.630
We want to find out the
total score per team.

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.910
So right now, this is a
pretty simple pipeline.

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:12.290
And let's see what
happens if we execute it.

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:14.690
So in this looping
animation, again, you

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:17.800
see our axes of event
time and processing time.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:21.130
And that gray line
represents processing time

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:23.040
as it progresses.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.870
As the pipeline executes
and encounters elements,

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:28.180
they're accumulated into
that intermediate state,

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:29.990
shown in white.

00:24:29.990 --> 00:24:32.600
And finally, when
processing completes,

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.340
we emit the result in yellow.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:37.470
So this is just how a batch
pipeline would execute.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:38.840
It's pretty standard.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:40.960
But as we address some of
the next few questions,

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:43.694
this is going to
get more powerful.

00:24:43.694 --> 00:24:45.360
The first thing we're
going to play with

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.450
is this notion of event time.

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:50.610
So we'll start by specifying
a windowing function.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:53.370
By doing this, we can
calculate independent results

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:55.450
for different slices
of event time.

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:58.880
So we could compute something
for every minute, every hour,

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:00.170
every day.

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:02.850
In this case, we're going to
use two-minute chunks of time

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:07.040
and ask for a different sum
per team every two minutes.

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:10.090
So now if we look at
how things execute,

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:12.230
you can see that
based on event time,

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:14.550
we're calculating four
independent results.

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:17.030
Every two-minute
slice of event time

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:20.530
is going to give us
a different result.

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:22.500
Now, at this point,
we're still waiting

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:25.150
for the entire
computation to complete

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:27.544
before we emit any results.

00:25:27.544 --> 00:25:28.960
And this works OK
if we're dealing

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:31.430
with a bounded, fixed-size
data set that will eventually

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:32.712
finish processing.

00:25:32.712 --> 00:25:34.170
But it's not going
to work if we're

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:36.610
processing an infinite
amount of data,

00:25:36.610 --> 00:25:41.250
because we'll never actually
complete and produce results.

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:44.290
So there, what we need to
do is reduce the latency

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:46.400
of individuals results.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:48.820
And we do that by
asking for results

00:25:48.820 --> 00:25:51.980
to be triggered-- triggered
based on the system's best

00:25:51.980 --> 00:25:54.050
estimate of data completion.

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:56.269
We call this estimate
the watermark.

00:25:56.269 --> 00:25:57.810
It's the way the
system estimates it.

00:25:57.810 --> 00:25:59.920
At this point, it's
pretty much seen

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:04.350
all the data it's going to see
for this portion of event time.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:08.010
So now I've drawn the watermark
on our graph in green.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.890
And you can see that as
the results for each window

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:14.130
passes the watermark and we
think we've pretty much got all

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:17.490
the data, we go ahead
and admit the result.

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:21.000
But again, this watermark
is just a heuristic.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:24.000
It's the system's best guess
about data completeness,

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:25.840
and guessing in a
distributed system

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:27.730
is going to be a bad idea.

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:30.020
Right now, the water
mark is too fast.

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:32.310
We're moving on before
we have all the data.

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:35.120
So in that first window,
we thought we were done.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.520
But then that user who was
offline in the elevator

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:39.280
finally reconnects,
and we realize

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:42.479
we've got some late data, and it
just gets missed in this case.

00:26:42.479 --> 00:26:43.520
That user is out of luck.

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:47.010
Their team is never going
to benefit from that score.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.210
Now, we could be slower
and more conservative

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:50.420
with our watermark.

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.180
But if we do that,
we might have to wait

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:53.660
an unrealistic amount of time.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:57.065
So if we're dealing with those
offline users on the airplane,

00:26:57.065 --> 00:26:58.940
we're going to have to
wait till every flight

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:02.310
everywhere lands just in case
there was somebody in seat 26b

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:04.120
who was playing our game.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:05.890
So that's not
reasonable, either.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:09.220
We need to be able to deal
with this type of heuristic,

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:13.450
best effort watermark
and work around it.

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:17.310
So we do that by using a
more sophisticated trigger.

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:21.380
So now we're requesting both
early speculative firings--

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:23.710
so as results are
trickling in, we're

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:27.300
asking for tentative
updates as we go.

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:30.990
And also, we're asking for late
firings or additional updates

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:34.302
if we see events
later than we expect.

00:27:34.302 --> 00:27:36.010
So what this means,
though, is that we're

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:39.100
going to be seeing multiple
results for that same slice

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:40.450
of event time.

00:27:40.450 --> 00:27:43.270
And now we have to answer
our fourth question-- how do

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:46.450
those results refine over time?

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:48.610
In this case, we're going
to use accumulation mode

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:50.430
and just continually
accumulate the scores.

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:52.300
So every time we
see new results,

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:57.750
we'll emit a new cumulative sum
that just continues to grow.

00:27:57.750 --> 00:27:59.460
Now we go back to
our graph, and you

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:01.400
can see that we're
getting multiple results

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:04.160
for each slice of event time.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:06.460
In some windows,
like the second,

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:08.570
we've produced some
early speculative results

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:10.400
as data is still arriving.

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:13.320
Then in every window,
as the watermark passes,

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:15.010
we think we've pretty
much seen it all,

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.020
and we emit an on time result.

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:19.270
And finally, if there
are late results,

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:22.740
like in that first window,
we might omit a late updated

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:23.770
element.

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:26.050
And because we
chose to accumulate,

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:28.870
each result that we omit
includes all the elements

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:31.490
for that window, even if
those elements were already

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:38.630
part of a previous result.

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:41.130
So what we did here is
we took an algorithm.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:43.970
In this case, it was just
simple integer summation.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:46.340
I could have taken a more
complicated algorithm,

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:48.340
except then I would
have had to animate it,

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:50.346
and these already got
complicated enough.

00:28:50.346 --> 00:28:51.720
So we just did
integer sums here,

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:54.230
but it could be a much
more powerful chunk

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:56.220
of user application logic.

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:58.120
And then we tweaked a few lines.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:01.260
And by changing those other
questions and addressing them,

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:03.840
we were able to cover a
whole number of use cases.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.540
We went from traditional
batch all the way up

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.540
through advanced
streaming without having

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:10.960
to change anything about
our algorithm, the what

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:13.860
that we're actually computing.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:16.900
So just like MapReduce
fundamentally changed the way

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.630
that we do data
processing by providing

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.690
a new set of abstractions
that allowed us

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:24.430
to reason about these
types of programs,

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:25.850
we think this data
flow model will

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:28.110
change the way we do
batch and streaming

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:31.000
processing in the future
by unifying them together

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:34.262
based on these four questions.

00:29:34.262 --> 00:29:36.220
So that's the first
benefit of Cloud Dataflow--

00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:38.250
the programming
model and the way

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:40.870
that you write your pipelines.

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:43.250
The second part is the
fully managed service

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:45.590
for executing these pipelines.

00:29:45.590 --> 00:29:48.490
This service scales the number
of workers and configures

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:50.900
that throughout pipeline
execution as your data

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:52.600
sizes evolve.

00:29:52.600 --> 00:29:56.952
It dynamically schedules work
across your different machines,

00:29:56.952 --> 00:29:59.035
and it provides integrated
monitoring and logging.

00:30:02.850 --> 00:30:06.540
So between those two--
between the model

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:08.280
and the fully-managed
service-- we've

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:10.440
addressed two of
our three goals,

00:30:10.440 --> 00:30:14.040
but we still want to
address portability.

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:18.510
That's where Apache
Beam comes in.

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:22.225
So in late January, we took
the dataflow model and the SDKs

00:30:22.225 --> 00:30:25.050
that implement it, and we
developed these in conjunction

00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:27.150
with the Manage
Dataflow service.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:30.380
But along with some partners,
we took this programming model

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:33.700
and donated it to the
Apache Software Foundation

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:37.610
as the incubating project
Apache Beam, which means,

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:39.270
among other things,
we get a new logo.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:41.650
So here's our snazzy
new logo with two

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:44.680
overlapping beams of light
representing the unification

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:48.580
of batch and stream into beam.

00:30:48.580 --> 00:30:50.390
And now we have puns all over.

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.550
We're beaming with joy.

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:54.910
The puns never get old.

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:57.900
But what does Apache
Beam actually include?

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.150
So it's the programming
model, this idea

00:31:00.150 --> 00:31:02.500
of describing your
data processing

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:07.040
in terms of the what,
where, when, and how.

00:31:07.040 --> 00:31:09.520
It's the initial job
SDK that we developed

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:10.990
as part of Cloud Dataflow.

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:15.850
And there will be other SDKs,
including Python to follow.

00:31:15.850 --> 00:31:18.020
And most importantly,
for portability,

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:19.610
it's the number of
runners that know

00:31:19.610 --> 00:31:22.260
how to take a beam
pipeline and execute it

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.980
on an existing
distributed back end.

00:31:24.980 --> 00:31:26.840
So right now, that
includes runners

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:30.330
that can run a beam pipeline
on Apache Fling, Apache

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:32.880
Spark, and then of course,
the Cloud Dataflow service.

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:39.500
Our goal as we transition the
Dataflow programming model

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:41.340
into Beam over the
next few months

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:45.250
is to fully support three
different categories of users.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.499
We've always focused
on end users.

00:31:47.499 --> 00:31:49.040
These are the users
who want to write

00:31:49.040 --> 00:31:50.530
data-processing pipelines.

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:52.800
They want that nice, little
Pac-Man from the beginning

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:54.841
where they focus on their
logic and their results

00:31:54.841 --> 00:31:57.020
and they don't have to
deal with the other stuff.

00:31:57.020 --> 00:31:59.353
They just want to take the
language that they're already

00:31:59.353 --> 00:32:01.970
familiar with and run it in
whatever distributed processing

00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:05.160
environment is most
convenient for them.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:08.480
But in addition to those users,
now as part of Apache Beam,

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:13.050
we want to focus on developing
stable APIs and documentation

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:15.750
that are going to allow others
in the open source community

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:18.760
to develop new SDKs
and provide new runners

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:21.817
for alternative distributed
processing environments.

00:32:21.817 --> 00:32:24.025
So we're just getting started
growing that community.

00:32:28.490 --> 00:32:31.180
JAMES MALONE: So building a
community around Apache Beam

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:34.780
is going to be absolutely
critical to its success.

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:38.270
The community will help
Apache Beam really achieve

00:32:38.270 --> 00:32:41.670
the goal of being an open source
platform for next generation

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:43.480
data processing.

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:46.870
But building a community
involves a few key things.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:49.400
And we're just getting started
building this community.

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:52.810
First, we have to
focus on collaboration.

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:56.680
We were really surprised
when we wrote the Apache Beam

00:32:56.680 --> 00:32:58.560
incubating proposal
and submitted it.

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:02.695
There was an outstanding amount
of interest in Apache Beam.

00:33:02.695 --> 00:33:04.070
There's also a
lot of groups that

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:06.180
have thrown in
new ideas and ways

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:09.230
that they want to expand
Beam and see Beam grow.

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:11.610
Taking all of this and
collaborating together

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:14.970
so we can take all of the
collective energy and brain

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:17.100
power and needs and
problems that people

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:20.350
want to solve and work together
towards a unified solution

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:22.960
is going to be both a
challenge, but ultimately,

00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:25.830
incredibly rewarding.

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:29.430
As Frances mentioned, the
data flow SDK and model

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:31.839
at this point has really
focused on end users.

00:33:31.839 --> 00:33:33.505
We want Beam, through
the collaboration,

00:33:33.505 --> 00:33:38.750
to also include the SDK
writers and the runner writers.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:41.130
Now, we want to
grow Apache Beam.

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:44.450
And there's a few people
that may be wondering, why

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:45.920
the Apache Software Foundation.

00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:49.590
It's come up in conversations
that I've had with people here.

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:52.330
Ultimately, Apache
Beam is a great fit

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:53.810
for the Apache
Software Foundation

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:56.420
because it will grow within
a community of other data

00:33:56.420 --> 00:33:57.770
processing tools.

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:01.130
So as Francis mentioned, there's
a Flink runner and a Spark

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:02.270
runner.

00:34:02.270 --> 00:34:06.130
It's not all about just making
Apache Beam awesome and better.

00:34:06.130 --> 00:34:08.795
There may be things that we
need to help out on with Spark

00:34:08.795 --> 00:34:10.920
or with Flink or other projects.

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:13.469
And as a member of the
Apache software community

00:34:13.469 --> 00:34:16.040
and under that umbrella,
we can make that happen.

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:19.040
And that's critically important.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:21.210
We also want to learn.

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:25.239
And this is Google's first
data-related contribution

00:34:25.239 --> 00:34:27.300
to the Apache
Software Foundation.

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.620
And we're learning quite a bit.

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:31.449
We're learning the Apache way.

00:34:31.449 --> 00:34:34.190
We're also shifting from
Google tools and infrastructure

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:36.147
to those that are used
by Apache Projects.

00:34:36.147 --> 00:34:37.730
And we're trying to
take that learning

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:40.890
and apply it, and learn quickly,
and get started quickly,

00:34:40.890 --> 00:34:43.960
and move things forward.

00:34:43.960 --> 00:34:47.389
So we wrote the
proposal in January,

00:34:47.389 --> 00:34:50.590
and we entered incubation
to the Apache incubator

00:34:50.590 --> 00:34:52.800
in early February.

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:55.179
To show how serious we are
about really getting started

00:34:55.179 --> 00:34:57.550
and making an impact and
moving things forward,

00:34:57.550 --> 00:34:59.260
the first commit
to the repository

00:34:59.260 --> 00:35:00.610
was in late February.

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:04.430
So we're really
excited to get moving.

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:06.970
Early in 2016-- so
where we are now--

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:10.160
we're really focused on
refactoring and redesign.

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:13.210
We really, really want to
focus on all three of those

00:35:13.210 --> 00:35:17.310
use cases-- so the end users,
the runner writers, and the SDK

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:18.500
writers.

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:21.610
That means by mid-year, we're
going to take all of the design

00:35:21.610 --> 00:35:24.470
and refactoring and things that
we've come up with, new APIs,

00:35:24.470 --> 00:35:26.100
and we're going to
be implementing them

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:28.150
on the Beam project.

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:30.810
It's important to note,
though, that we don't want to

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:32.550
and we will not
interrupt data flow

00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:34.050
users who are in this process.

00:35:34.050 --> 00:35:37.170
So we're really focused
on improving and making

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:39.400
these updates to
the Beam project.

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:43.320
So towards the end of
the year, the Beam team

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:45.970
can say that Beam pipelines
will run on multiple runners.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:48.070
You could take a Beam
pipeline and run it reliably

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:49.570
on Flink or Spark.

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:52.384
You could also use the Beam SDK
to articulate Beam pipelines

00:35:52.384 --> 00:35:54.175
and run them on the
Cloud Dataflow service.

00:35:56.980 --> 00:36:00.200
So with Beam and
Cloud Dataflow, you

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:03.720
start to check all of the
boxes for next generation data

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:04.690
processing.

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:08.190
With Cloud Dataflow, you get
an awesome managed service

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:10.690
where you can send it
pipelines and you don't

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:12.630
worry about infrastructure.

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:14.640
You don't worry about
spinning up servers.

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:17.000
You don't worry about
logging and monitoring.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:21.380
With Beam, you get a portable,
powerful, unified model

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:24.580
that you can use to articulate
data processing pipelines.

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:27.150
So between the two,
you start to end up

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:29.135
in a place that is
truly next generation.

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:34.290
That said, while we think
that Cloud Dataflow is going

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:36.420
to be an awesome place
to run Beam pipelines,

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:39.240
and we think it will
be the best-managed way

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.530
to run Beam pipelines, Beam
itself is also portable,

00:36:42.530 --> 00:36:44.200
which means that there
are other runners

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:45.630
that you can use to run Beam.

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:48.850
And these runners can exist
in any number of environments.

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:50.570
They might be on premise.

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:52.690
You might want to run
Flink, for instance,

00:36:52.690 --> 00:36:55.230
on a cluster in your
local data center.

00:36:55.230 --> 00:36:57.570
You might want to run
Spark on another cloud.

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:59.640
That's OK.

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:02.120
These runners may have
varying levels of fidelity

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:04.335
in how they support Beam
based on their own models

00:37:04.335 --> 00:37:06.020
and underlying semantics.

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:07.660
But again, going
back to my comment

00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:10.500
about being a member of the
Apache Software Foundation,

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:12.170
that's why it's very
important for us

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:14.900
to work together inside
of the Apache umbrella

00:37:14.900 --> 00:37:18.130
so we can try and help everybody
out and move everybody forward.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:23.450
Now, it's getting late.

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:26.050
And if you only remember
a few things from this--

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:27.300
we've been talking for awhile.

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:29.091
If you wake up tomorrow,
and you were like,

00:37:29.091 --> 00:37:31.927
that was the best presentation
I've ever seen, please remember

00:37:31.927 --> 00:37:33.135
it with the following points.

00:37:35.870 --> 00:37:38.390
First, the MapReduce
paper in 2004

00:37:38.390 --> 00:37:40.860
kicked off a flurry of activity.

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:43.440
You had two branches that
predominately emerged.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.940
You had one in
Google that continued

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:46.860
to iterate on the
ideas of MapReduce

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:48.620
and create new
papers, and then you

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:52.880
had a whole ecosystem develop
in the open source community.

00:37:52.880 --> 00:37:54.336
As a result of
these two branches,

00:37:54.336 --> 00:37:56.460
we have two awesome products
that you can use today

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:58.600
in Google Cloud Platform
to take advantage

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:01.422
of the work in these
two work streams.

00:38:01.422 --> 00:38:03.130
You have Cloud Dataflow
to take advantage

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:05.380
of all of the awesome iteration
in both infrastructure

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:07.740
and software that Google
invested after MapReduce,

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:10.152
and you have Cloud
Dataproc to take advantage

00:38:10.152 --> 00:38:13.050
of all of those components you
could use on Cloud Dataproc

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:14.860
today.

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:18.470
Apache Beam is a bridge
between these two work streams

00:38:18.470 --> 00:38:19.860
that split in 2004.

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:23.460
In 2016, we want to
bring them back together.

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:25.740
Apache Beam can be managed.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:28.070
Using services like
Dataflow, it's very easy

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:30.220
run Beam on Managed Services.

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:34.400
Beam provides a unified
model for batch and stream

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:35.940
processing.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:39.090
Finally, Beam
pipelines are portable

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:40.270
along different runners.

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:42.190
You can take your Beam
pipelines and run them

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:44.655
on different runners
in different locations.

00:38:49.497 --> 00:38:50.455
FRANCES PERRY: Am I on?

00:38:50.455 --> 00:38:50.760
There we go.

00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:52.259
We hope this talk
leaves you wanting

00:38:52.259 --> 00:38:54.050
to learn more about
doing data processing

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:55.820
and open source at Google.

00:38:55.820 --> 00:38:57.950
You can go use Cloud
Dataproc today.

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:00.410
You can start spinning up
Hadoop and Spark clusters

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:02.960
and use that
fully-managed service.

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:06.550
If you want to learn about the
dataflow-- now the Beam model--

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:09.410
you can use Dataflow
today to write pipelines

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:11.270
that run on Cloud Dataflow.

00:39:11.270 --> 00:39:13.980
And over time, as we
finish bootstrapping,

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:16.330
you'll be able to use
Apache Beam to run

00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:20.070
those same pipelines anywhere.

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:21.760
Well, thank you very much.

00:39:21.760 --> 00:39:23.510
James and I will be
around, so please come

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:24.468
find us, ask questions.

00:39:24.468 --> 00:39:25.340
Thank you very much.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:28.390
[APPLAUSE]

